WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.430
Welcome back to the David Pakman show. It's
great to welcome to the program today. Sheri

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.960
Berman, who's a professor of Political Science
Barnard College

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.200
at Columbia University. Most recent book is
democracy and dictatorship in Europe from

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.090
the, and I hope I'm pronouncing it correctly,
the NCCN regime to the present day. Is that

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:21.840
how it's said? That's perfect. Good. Okay.
Uh, all right, so let's, so let's jump right

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.039
into it. We've been talking a lot about, um,
social democracy in the context of what are

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:33.239
the differences between that and democratic
socialism. How does this compare to different

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:39.100
types of capitalism? And one of the interesting
areas of this is that much of sort of modern

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:47.489
social democracy, socially democratic thought
stems from uh, early 20th century Sweden and

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:53.399
the Social Democratic Party. And if we look
today at Europe and what's going on, that

00:00:53.399 --> 00:01:00.289
model has actually changed significantly with
the rise in right wing nationalism in Europe

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:04.570
and a lot of other changes that are taking
place. So how would you, if someone just says

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:09.520
to you, you know, Sherry, how has the state
of social democracy changed over the last

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.060
Hundred Years in Europe? How would you generally
characterize it?

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.189
Well, so there's lots of different ways to
answer that question, which is never a good

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:21.750
answer to a question. I mean, as far as the
sort of status of parties that call themselves

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.430
social democratic or would be seen as kind
of center left, we have seen a dramatic decline

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:33.439
in support for these parties over the late
20th and early 21st century. So one way to

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:38.210
tell this story is to talk about the rise
of these parties electorally, politically

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:44.540
during the early 20th century. There sort
of greatest electoral success after the Second

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.211
World War and then a decline that again has
set in over the last decades. Um, the other

00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:55.479
way to tell the story is by focusing less
on how many votes they get and more on their

00:01:55.479 --> 00:02:01.020
ideas and how powerful those ideas have been.
The traditionally or those sort of distinctive

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:08.510
social democratic view is one that accepts
capitalism, but as wary of it and believes

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:14.870
that it is necessary for the democratic state
to intervene in markets and in the economic

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:22.760
sphere in general to sort of protect citizens
from the most destructive and destabilizing

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.370
consequences of markets.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:29.160
And again, if we're going to look at it that
way, then one could say I'm starting in the

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.550
20th. The beginning of the 20th century Social
Democrats within left started fighting for

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:41.040
this view against folks further to the left
Marxist Communists, Democratic Socialists,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.780
um, only in Scandinavia and particularly in
Sweden where these kinds of social democratic

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:52.450
use able to triumph on the left during the
interwar period. But after the Second World

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:58.370
War, these type of use come to dominate mainstream
center left and social democratic parties

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:04.460
as well as by the way most other parties in
Europe on the center and center. Right. So

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:10.650
this basic social democratic view that it
was the task of democratic states to control

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:17.650
capitalism to or rather to tame capitalism,
to protect citizens. This was basically accepted

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:23.290
across the board in Europe after the Second
World War. But by the end of the 20th century,

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:29.780
this consensus begins to fray and in fact
becomes largely replaced by a more neo liberal

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:36.240
consensus which rains, uh, you know, sort
of up through, I would say the financial and

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.730
euro crisis. And now we're back again to an
era when that consensus, that more neoliberal

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.570
consensus is being questioned. And we're again
debating some fundamental questions about

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:52.480
the relationship between democracy and capitalism,
the correct role and scope for markets, things

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.560
of that nature.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:59.650
Is there a, let me rephrase it a different
way. Are there places in Europe where, uh,

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:06.900
uh, as as an, as a sort of idea labeled as
social democracy, many of those ideas are

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.040
just sort of mainstream and as such, it's
not as important that they be labeled in that

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.660
way. So when you look at, for example, the
healthcare systems of many European countries,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:22.660
the debate over, well it's the Social Democrats
who want one type of system and they're pushing

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.040
against everybody else who wants to sort of
more American type system, aren't a lot of

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.080
those, um, uh, institutions now just kind
of part of the baseline. And as such, there

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.440
are no longer labeled as socially democratic.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.590
So I mean, that is absolutely correct. Unlike
in the United States, citizens in Europe since

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:47.590
1945 have come to take for granted a whole
range of social protections that we are still

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.530
fighting over here in the United States. And
you mentioned the most obvious ones, universal

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:59.040
healthcare, free education, a whole variety
of other social protections that we do not

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.040
have as a matter of citizenship here in the
u s in that sense, a sort of social democratic

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:10.090
view of the relationship between states and
markets still reigns. But these kinds of systems

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:16.910
have come under stress in Europe. Um, and
they have been changed and they have changed

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:23.160
fairly dramatically over the past decade.
So while you still have a very extensive welfare

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.910
state, for example, in Europe and while in
places like Sweden, you know, you still have

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:33.190
high levels of government spending, everybody
expects healthcare, pensions, education, et

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:38.700
Cetera to be given. As a matter of course,
you have seen significant privatization of

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:44.090
many of these policies. You've seen rising
inequality and you've seen some deterioration

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:49.040
around the edge in the kinds of commitments
states have made to citizens. So yes, um,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.020
no. In Europe it is generally accepted that,
um, you know, welfare states you are a good

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.210
thing and that governments do need to provide
social protection for citizens. But there

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:04.450
has been an erosion of many of these policies
and more importantly, perhaps many of the

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:09.530
principles upon which they were predicated
after the Second World War.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:15.331
So, um, in any election, there is always what
we would call some level of defection, people

00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:19.860
who in a primary vote on one side and a nut
voting for in the general election for someone

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.230
from a different party there. There's the
affection of all kinds, but we have some data

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:32.010
specifically that 12% of Bernie Primary voters
in 2016 ended up voting for Donald Trump.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.260
Now they were, you can argue balanced out
by some, uh, you know, ted Cruz voters who

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:39.580
ended up voting for Hillary Clinton. But to
focus in specifically for the purposes of

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:47.870
our conversation on those 12%, is that a phenomenon
that is also identifiable in Europe where

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:53.860
European Social Democrats and folks more aligned
with the ideology of Bernie Sanders gets sort

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:58.550
of swept up in some of these populist right
wing movements or in the nationalism and end

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:03.490
up supporting that or, or is that not a dynamic
you've observed in Europe?

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:10.260
So the answer to your question again is yes
and no. Okay. I mean, look at any, um, American

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:16.700
election, the candidate in the primaries,
who, what the supporters of primary candidates

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:23.370
who lose some percentage of them always defect
the other party. So the 12% of Bernie Sanders

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:28.280
voters who defected to Trump is not out of
line with the number of Clinton supporters

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:33.889
who defected after Clinton lost to Obama.
But the larger point that you raise is a very

00:07:33.889 --> 00:07:41.419
important one. The defection of lower educated
and working class white voters from the Democratic

00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:48.290
Party to the Republican Party. And particularly
to Trump in 2016 this is a broader phenomenon

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:54.060
that we have seen in Europe. In fact, in many
European countries now it is not social democratic

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.449
parties, but right wing populous parties that
are the largest working class parties in their

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:03.810
party systems. That is to say more workers,
people in the working class support right

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:08.560
wing populist parties in many European countries,
including France and Austria. Then they do

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:14.950
Social Democratic Party. So the broader phenomenon,
right? Uh, folks defecting from the left,

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:19.060
particularly again among the working class
and people with lower educational levels to

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:24.550
the populist. Right. This is a profoundly
important and he stabilizing phenomenon that

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.630
you can see on both sides of the Atlantic.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:31.419
Hmm. Um, in the book sort of related to this,
one of the things we talk about our countries

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:37.479
in Europe that have formed, uh, I guess what
we would call liberal Western democracies

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.560
and the last hundreds, several hundred years,
and that at different points in time and for

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.980
different reasons, there's sort of a backsliding
that happens towards autocracy towards authoritarianism.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:52.740
Um, what are the more common reasons, if there
are unifying, are common reasons that that

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.560
happens? Or are they really unique cases?

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.680
Well, there's lots of reasons why they happen
and I think it is important given how worried

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:06.340
we are about the state of democracy today
to point out that we still have more democracies

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:11.120
in the world today than ever before, and that
we've had less backsliding after the wave

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.440
of democratization that began in the late
20th century with the collapse of the Soviet

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.200
Union. Then we have during previous democratic
wave. So those would be waves in 1848 after

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.690
the first world war, after the Second World
War. So overall the state of democracy is

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:30.620
still quite strong. But for countries that
do backslid in places where democracy does

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:36.260
decay if not collapse, there are definitely
some patterns that um, you know we should

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:43.570
be wary of because in the past they have led
to again, significant problems with democracy,

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:48.339
social polarization, which of course is something
we are constantly talking about here in the

00:09:48.339 --> 00:09:54.010
u s this is very problematic because it makes
democracy almost impossible to function.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.990
People see their opponents is illegitimate
rather than just as opponents. They find it

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.950
very difficult to compromise and bargain,
which of course is what democracy requires.

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:08.330
Every political battle becomes zero sum and
so it's very difficult to have well functioning

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:12.630
democracies and the kinds of situations where
you have social polarization parties are pushed

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:17.070
further to the extreme, that kind of thing.
In general, if we think about the sort of

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:22.500
period when democracy, quote unquote work
best in the west, that is to say after the

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:27.190
Second World War when we got stable, well
functioning democracies in western Europe

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:32.480
as well as obviously well functioning democracy
here in the United States. We should also

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.940
remember that this was a period with very
particular social and economic characteristics

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:43.620
as well. We had rising growth. Europe grew
the fastest it's ever grown, um, in its entire

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:50.070
history in the 30 years after 1945 and along
with that growth came incredible equalization

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.550
of incomes and wealth.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:56.440
Um, again, greater equalization that had ever
occurred before. And this is important because

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:02.540
it helped to diminish divisions in society
and help to create the sense that the democratic

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:06.670
system could deliver for everyone. That the
gains of one group did not come at the expense

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:11.540
of the others and that politicians and parties
were responsive to the needs of their citizens.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:16.899
Of course, we all know that this kind of,
um, these kinds of developments changed dramatically

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:21.940
in the late 20th century when growth slowed,
unemployment rose, particularly in Europe,

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:26.240
inequality skyrocketed in the United States
in particular, but in almost all European

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:32.210
countries. Well, and we began to see, again,
greater divisions within Western societies,

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:38.150
all of which were, um, accompanied by dramatic
social and demographic change, which only

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:43.630
complicated the process of democratic politics,
even more [inaudible].

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.050
Absolutely fascinating stuff. And I think
anybody who has listened to our conversation

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:51.770
can tell that there is a lot to be gleaned
from the history of Europe in terms of what,

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:56.740
uh, we are starting to see in some ways in
the United States today. The book is democracy

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:01.860
and dictatorship in Europe from the onsie
on regime to the present day. We've been speaking

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:02.860
with the books.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:07.680
They're Barnard College Professor Sheri Berman.
Sherri, really a pleasure having you on. Thank

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.060
you so much. I was really glad to have this
conversation.

